 "beats": [
  {
   "type": "title",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "globe",
   "text": "How did the Ottomans get into Europe?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Across this narrow strait, the Dardanelles. Today it runs through Türkiye, between Europe and Asia."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "strait",
   "date": "Today",
   "place": "Dardanelles",
   "text": "Wasn't the Byzantine Empire guarding it?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "It was weak, and fighting a civil war."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "kantakouzenos",
   "date": "1340s",
   "place": "Byzantium",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.3,
     1.4
    ]
   },
   "text": "One rival emperor, John Kantakouzenos, allied with Orhan, gave him his daughter in marriage, and brought Ottoman soldiers into Europe to fight for him."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "quake",
   "date": "",
   "text": "So when did they stay for good?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "In 1354, an earthquake shook the walls of Gallipoli. Orhan's son Süleyman marched in, brought settlers from Anatolia, and never left."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "push",
   "text": "Just like that?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Right. Gallipoli became the bridgehead. In the 1360s they took Adrianople, today's Edirne, which became their new capital."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "edirne",
   "date": "1360s",
   "place": "Adrianople",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.55,
     0.55,
     1.25
    ]
   },
   "text": "From there, Sultan Murad pushed deep into the Balkans."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "lazar",
   "date": "",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.45,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.3,
     1.3
    ]
   },
   "text": "Didn't anyone stop them?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "Serbia tried. Prince Lazar gathered an army to meet Murad on the field of Kosovo."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "kosovo",
   "date": "15 June 1389",
   "place": "Kosovo field",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.3,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.6,
     0.5,
     1.25
    ]
   },
   "text": "On the fifteenth of June, 1389, the two armies clashed. Both rulers died that day."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "murad",
   "move": {
    "from": [
     0.5,
     0.5,
     1.0
    ],
    "to": [
     0.5,
     0.35,
     1.35
    ]
   },
   "text": "Legend says a Serbian knight reached Murad's tent and stabbed him. He is the only Ottoman sultan to die on a battlefield."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "So who won?"
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "scene": "legacy",
   "date": "",
   "text": "Not exactly clear. Both armies were shattered. But Serbia could not recover, and soon became an Ottoman vassal. Serbs still remember the battle every year on Vidovdan."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "scene": "push",
   "text": "So the Ottomans were in Europe to stay."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "a",
   "text": "For more than five hundred years. Next time: the sultan who was taken prisoner. Like the video and follow History Sketches."
  },
  {
   "type": "narration",
   "speaker": "q",
   "text": "And tell us in the comments what you think!"
  },
  {
   "type": "end",
   "text": "Next: the sultan taken prisoner",
   "sub": "Follow History Sketches"
  }
 ]
}